scope: "The `.lts` WRITER — one library, two components, two different binding invariants. (A) THE EMITTER: structured content -> `.lts` text, for authoring NEW content, bound by `parse(emit(x)) == x` and required to FAIL CLOSED rather than emit a best effort. (B) THE REWRITER: existing `.lts` text -> canonical `.lts` text, for migrating the legacy corpus, bound by `parse(rewrite(f)) >= parse(f)` (superset, never subset) and FORBIDDEN from being implemented as `emit(parse(f))`. Also: the transformation set and its per-transformation opt-in, the two-level verification (graph superset AND text-level line accounting), byte-level idempotence, the per-file receipt, the encoding-shape rules the emitter honours, and the absorption of `composeio.decision_ops`' byte-format writer plus `project_validator.render_registry_lts` into the one library."
audience: [the builder implementing the writer library, factlattice-maintainer, the operators of `decision_ops` / `defect_ops` / `project_validator` whose emitters are absorbed, factlattice-editor (a caller, and the agent that hand-types today), composeux-architect (ratifies before dispatch; no decision point in this document is open any longer), David (ruled the two-component split, the anti-round-trip prohibition, the emitter's input type and the no-reorder rule, all 2026-08-11), anyone who later asks why the rewriter was not simply parse-then-emit, why the emitter's input type is not a richer authoring model, or why the migration does not produce a canonical byte form]
elements: [CXC-LTW-*, DD-LTW-*]
boundary: "IN: the emitter and its round-trip invariant ([CXC-LTW-EMITTER]); the emitter's refusal contract ([CXC-LTW-EMIT-REFUSE]); the rewriter and its superset invariant ([CXC-LTW-REWRITER]); the PROHIBITION on implementing the rewriter as parse-then-emit, with the 18% measurement that is its evidence ([CXC-LTW-NO-ROUNDTRIP]); the two-level verification that a lossy parse forces ([CXC-LTW-VERIFY]); the five named transformations ([CXC-LTW-TRANSFORMS]); per-transformation opt-in ([CXC-LTW-OPTIN]); byte-level idempotence ([CXC-LTW-IDEMPOTENT]); the per-file receipt and the leave-untouched rule ([CXC-LTW-RECEIPT]); the encoding-shape rules ([CXC-LTW-SHAPE]) and the derive-do-not-invent rule for canonical form ([CXC-LTW-CANONICAL-DERIVED]); one library and its four callers ([CXC-LTW-ONE-WRITER], [CXC-LTW-CALLERS]); the parser dependency that gates implementation ([CXC-LTW-PARSER-DEP]); the error matrix and the two-tier acceptance bar. OUT: the corpus-wide migration campaign itself — this specs the TOOL, not the rollout ([CXC-LTW-OUT-OF-SCOPE]); any change to the parser, which is being fixed concurrently and is this spec's DEPENDENCY, not its content; any change to the document-root and `elements` rules, which are [SPEC:LTS-DOC-ROOT]'s and are CONSUMED here unchanged."
not-this: "Not a second emitter. The corpus already has two live ones — `composeio.decision_ops.core.render_node` and `intelligence/tools/project_validator.py::render_registry_lts` — and adding a third beside them is the fork-divergence class this corpus keeps paying for; both are ABSORBED ([CXC-LTW-ONE-WRITER]). Not a formatter with a house style: the canonical form is DERIVED from what conformant files already do, with `29-composeux-embedded-reactive.lts` as the exemplar, because a writer that reformats 72 files to a new taste produces a diff nobody can review ([CXC-LTW-CANONICAL-DERIVED]). Not `emit(parse(f))` — that implementation is the obvious one and it is catastrophic, and the prohibition at [CXC-LTW-NO-ROUNDTRIP] is the single most important requirement in this document. Not implementable today: the parser fix for wrapped continuations and ID tables MUST land first, or the emitter encodes today's truncation as intent ([CXC-LTW-PARSER-DEP]). Not a richer authoring model: David ruled 2026-08-11 that the emitter's input type is the PARSER'S NODE MODEL, because that makes `parse(emit(x)) == x` a literal equality rather than an equality-after-projection, and a projection is a step where a loss can hide ([DD-LTW-INPUT-TYPE]). Not a canonicalizing rewriter: the rewriter transforms IN PLACE and does not reorder, because reordering cannot be undone once the pre-reorder byte layout and its blame history are gone, and a reordering diff defeats the human review gate on the very first run over 72 files ([DD-LTW-REORDER]). Both decision points are CLOSED; what still gates DISPATCH is the parser fix alone ([CXC-LTW-PARSER-DEP])."
