**Version**: 0.2
**Created**: 2026-08-11
**Status**: Draft — BOTH DECISION POINTS CLOSED; dispatch now gated on ONE external event only. (1) **[DD-LTW-INPUT-TYPE] is CLOSED — the emitter's input type is THE PARSER'S NODE MODEL** (branch a). `parse(emit(x)) == x` is the tool's entire reason to exist, and against the parser's own model it is a LITERAL equality; a richer authoring model makes it equality-AFTER-PROJECTION, and the projection is a step where a loss can hide — which is exactly the defect class this session spent itself finding. The richer model's real benefit was "the refusal set becomes a measured list of what authors are trying to say and cannot", and the REFUSAL LOG already yields that dataset as evidence rather than on speculation; LTW-C21 now requires refusals to be RECORDED so that substitute is real, because the ruling's `invalidating_assumption` is that the log actually gets read. The rejected branch is kept at full strength in the node. (2) **[DD-LTW-REORDER] is CLOSED — TRANSFORM IN PLACE, no reordering** (branch b), which RATIFIES the spec's existing default rather than changing it. In-place can become canonical later with one more pass; reordering CANNOT be undone, because the pre-reorder byte layout and its blame history are gone — and the first migration run is gated by a human reading a diff, which a reordering diff defeats at exactly the moment it matters most, on the very first run over 72 files. The rejected branch — a genuinely canonical form making byte comparison a valid equality test and idempotence trivial to prove — is kept at full strength, and its `invalidating_assumption` names the condition that would reopen it: a rewriter trusted enough to run unreviewed, proven by receipts. (3) The remaining gate is NOT a decision: implementation is BLOCKED on the concurrent parser fix for wrapped continuations and ID tables ([CXC-LTW-PARSER-DEP]) — an emitter written against today's reader would encode the truncation as intent, because its round trip would be satisfied by both sides truncating identically. The spec is ratifiable now; the build waits, and LTW-C0 is the blocking behavioural fixture that proves the dependency landed. The central requirement of this document is [CXC-LTW-NO-ROUNDTRIP]: the rewriter MUST NOT be `emit(parse(f))`, evidenced by `57-substrate-agnostic-subscription-filtering.lts` capturing 18% of its authored bytes into properties — round-tripping it would yield a file containing 18% of the original while every check passed, because both sides of the comparison would have passed through the lossy step. That prohibition forces the two-level verification at [CXC-LTW-VERIFY], and LTW-C6 is the acceptance criterion that requires the harness to demonstrate it can DETECT the prohibited implementation on the real file. One follow-up outside this document: the element prefix `CXC-LTW-` resolves to the registered first segment `CXC` ([PREFIX-CXC]) and needs no profile change, but the family is new and its sub-family line in that node's `definition` should gain `CXC-LTW-` when the profile is next touched.
**Intelligence**: composed by `factlattice-editor` from David's ratified design narrative, 2026-08-11. Grounded against `python/composeio/decision_ops/core.py` (the absorbed byte-format writer — `render_node`, `_format_value`, `find_node_span`, `_apply_with_guard`, `CANONICAL_FIELD_ORDER`), `python/composeio/defect_ops/core.py` (which already imports five of those symbols verbatim across a package boundary), `intelligence/tools/project_validator.py::render_registry_lts` (the second live emitter, with its existing render -> parse -> validate selftest), the exemplar `29-composeux-embedded-reactive.lts`, the sibling contract `58-document-root-and-element-inflation.lts`, the measured worst case `57-substrate-agnostic-subscription-filtering.lts`, and the `.lts` domain profile `corpora/intelligence-console/platform-composeio-profile.lts` for every relationship verb used above. Authored under its own rule S1: every property value in this document is on one physical line.
